== Dirac Bra-ket notation for conditional laws <bra-ket-section>

We will borrow Dirac's bra-ket notation and apply it to probability laws. The motivation came from a
need for a saner looking formula for the Chapman-Kolmogorov equations. As this is not a standard
presentation, we lay out the language and notation in this section.

#meta-prop[A probability space $(Omega, PP)$ and random variables $X,Y,Z : Omega -> D$.]
#let dom = $op("dom")$

We first define the product of a random variable $X$ and a probability measure $PP$ as the measure
$X dot.c PP$ acting on sets by $ forall B in dom(PP): (X dot.c PP) (B) := integral_B X dif PP. $

Now we can consider the restriction of that measure to the $sigma$-algebra generated by some random
variable $Y$, denoted by $X dot PP |_Y$. Clearly if $PP$ vanishes on some set, then so does $X dot
PP|_Y$ because multiplication of a finite number ($X$) with zero is still zero; that is,  the
absolute continuity condition $ X dot PP|_Y << PP $ is satisfied and so an almost-everywhere (in
$D$) defined Radon-Nikodym derivative exists $ (dif X dot PP|_Y)/(dif PP) (y) =: EE[X | Y = y] : D oto RR, $ called
the conditional expectation.

By the relation $PP(B) = EE [1_B]$ it then induces a conditional probability $ forall A in calB: PP(X in A | Y) := EE[1_(X in A) | Y], $ so $PP(X in A | Y=y)$ is defined for almost every $y in D$.

If we now let both $A in calB(D)$ and $y in D$ vary we get an object that we denote by 
$ cPPP(X given Y) &: calB(D) times D oto [0,1]
\ (A, y) &|-> PP(X in A | Y = y), $
where the $y$ dependence is still _almost_ sure.

So $cPPP(X given Y)$ is the conditional law of $X$ given $Y$. Let us then write the unconditional
law of a variable $Z$ as simply $PPP(Z)$.

As a positive function of $y in D$, $PP(X in A | Y = y)$ admits an integral against other measures,
so it is well-defined to integrate it against the law of the variable $Z$:
$ integral cPPP(X in A given Y=y) dPPP(Z=y). $
Let us denote this number by $ PPP( X in A | Y = y ranglee Z = y), $ where we treat $y$ as a bound
variable within the expression and write it only to make it clear what is the integrand ($Y$) and what is
the integrator ($Z$). Alternatively, we can omit writing the variable $y$ whatsoever and denote the
same quantity by just $ PPP( X in A | Y ranglee Z). $
If we let $A in calB$ vary, we get a back the conditional law of $X$ given $Y$ integrated against
$Z$ and denote it simply by $ PPP( X | Y ranglee Z) : calB(D) -> [0,1]. $

